{
  "slug": "artist:dr-dre",
  "name": "Dr. Dre",
  "editorial_article": "Dr. Dre began his trajectory in the 1980s as a member of World Class Wreckin' Cru before rising to prominence with N.W.A from 1987 to 1991. After leaving the group, he launched a solo career in 1992 under Death Row Records with the triple-platinum album The Chronic. The record featured key collaborations with Snoop Dogg on singles like 'Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang' and 'Fuck wit Dre Day'. In 1996, he founded his own imprint, Aftermath Entertainment, which served as the home for his second solo album, 1999's 2001. That project achieved six-times platinum certification and included hits such as 'Forgot About Dre' and 'The Next Episode'. During the 2000s, his work shifted heavily toward production for artists including Eminem, contributing to tracks like 'Encore' and 'Crack a Bottle'. His 2015 studio album Compton, which served as a soundtrack for the N.W.A biopic, debuted at number two on the Billboard 200.",
